WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.000 --> 00:00:07.504
The ways of the world often baffle me.

00:00:08.630 --> 00:00:12.884
I sometimes wonder if I missed the memo
about the most basic things.

00:00:12.926 --> 00:00:15.303
What are you supposed to make for dinner?

00:00:15.762 --> 00:00:18.348
What do you talk about in an elevator?

00:00:19.182 --> 00:00:21.309
Why do people cut in line?

00:00:22.185 --> 00:00:25.021
How do you leave a dinner party
without being rude?

00:00:25.063 --> 00:00:26.648
Or do you leave it all?

00:00:26.690 --> 00:00:28.316
["Why are you still here?"]

00:00:28.316 --> 00:00:30.026
(Laughter)

00:00:31.111 --> 00:00:34.489
My tendency to see the world
like I'm from outer space

00:00:34.489 --> 00:00:37.367
was a bit of a liability when I was a kid.

00:00:38.118 --> 00:00:39.286
True story.

00:00:39.286 --> 00:00:40.912
["What's a noogie?"]

00:00:40.954 --> 00:00:42.789
(Laughter)

00:00:42.831 --> 00:00:45.500
But it's been helpful in my career.

00:00:46.167 --> 00:00:47.544
I'm a cartoonist.

00:00:50.630 --> 00:00:55.176
When I first started making cartoons
for The New Yorker about a decade ago,

00:00:55.218 --> 00:00:57.846
I kept my ideas light and quirky.

00:00:58.805 --> 00:01:02.017
And please shout out
if you don't get any of them,

00:01:02.058 --> 00:01:03.226
I'll explain.

00:01:03.226 --> 00:01:06.229
(Laughter)

00:01:06.229 --> 00:01:08.732
["The synchronized swim team quits"]

00:01:08.773 --> 00:01:11.401
I didn't draw anything too personal.

00:01:11.443 --> 00:01:13.111
["Unicorns do exist!"]

00:01:13.153 --> 00:01:16.615
I mean, personal for someone.

00:01:17.782 --> 00:01:20.243
I figured I was too specific,

00:01:20.243 --> 00:01:24.039
too hard to relate to
and read, possibly, too female.

00:01:26.082 --> 00:01:27.584
It took a breakup.

00:01:27.626 --> 00:01:28.752
(Laughter)

00:01:29.336 --> 00:01:30.837
["I know there would be a time

00:01:30.879 --> 00:01:33.798
I could wear them
without destroying my feet."]

00:01:33.840 --> 00:01:34.966
(Laughter)

00:01:35.425 --> 00:01:39.804
It took a breakup to get me to start
drawing more autobiographically.

00:01:40.764 --> 00:01:45.518
The pain I was feeling, although
objectively pretty run of the mill,

00:01:45.560 --> 00:01:47.812
was impossible to ignore.

00:01:47.854 --> 00:01:49.105
[Tunnel of love]

00:01:49.147 --> 00:01:52.567
I knew that drying was my strongest
problem-solving tool.

00:01:53.818 --> 00:01:56.821
So I decided to diagram
what I was going through.

00:01:56.821 --> 00:01:58.990
[Tunnel of love.
Just kidding. Wall of death.]

00:01:59.032 --> 00:02:00.492
(Laughter)

00:02:01.910 --> 00:02:06.498
By making these drawings, I could see
how my ex and I had hurt each other.

00:02:07.624 --> 00:02:08.917
And move on.

00:02:11.086 --> 00:02:12.504
Onto other breakups.

00:02:12.545 --> 00:02:14.214
["Again?" Pit of despair]

00:02:14.255 --> 00:02:15.423
(Laughter)

00:02:16.591 --> 00:02:20.261
Drawing from my own life
was a revelation to me,

00:02:20.303 --> 00:02:23.890
not only because it helped me
understand myself better,

00:02:23.890 --> 00:02:26.267
but because it made me see
for the first time

00:02:26.309 --> 00:02:28.353
that people could relate to me.

00:02:30.772 --> 00:02:32.649
Now that I had this amazing tool,

00:02:32.649 --> 00:02:35.735
there were so many problems
I wanted to solve with it.

00:02:37.195 --> 00:02:38.947
The problem of scheduling.

00:02:38.947 --> 00:02:40.198
[Work | Family / Friends]

00:02:40.240 --> 00:02:44.244
The problem of too many things
happening all at the same time.

00:02:44.285 --> 00:02:47.455
[ "I'm having a birthday party!
"I'm visiting New York!"]

00:02:47.455 --> 00:02:51.668
The problem, relatedly, of time.

00:02:51.668 --> 00:02:53.044
[Childhood / now]

00:02:53.837 --> 00:02:56.715
And finally, dating again.

00:02:57.382 --> 00:02:58.508
(Laughter)

00:02:59.259 --> 00:03:01.928
There's an endless amount
to say about dating.

00:03:01.970 --> 00:03:05.098
(Laughter)

00:03:06.766 --> 00:03:08.601
There are, of course, problems

00:03:08.643 --> 00:03:11.521
that can't be summed up
in a single drawing.

00:03:12.814 --> 00:03:17.026
For these problems,
you need many drawings.

00:03:18.653 --> 00:03:22.949
One more complex problem
I have is with God.

00:03:24.451 --> 00:03:28.163
I'm Jewish, so I'm talking
about the God of the Old Testament.

00:03:29.205 --> 00:03:33.209
My problem with God
isn't actually a big problem.

00:03:33.251 --> 00:03:36.337
It's just, I don't know,
it's stayed with me.

00:03:36.379 --> 00:03:39.591
My problem with God
is that he's too confident.

00:03:39.591 --> 00:03:46.014
(Laughter)

00:03:47.557 --> 00:03:51.561
For me, creation is an act
of solving problems,

00:03:51.603 --> 00:03:53.271
of figuring things out.

00:03:54.439 --> 00:03:57.233
God already seems to have
everything figured out.

00:03:58.109 --> 00:04:01.613
He strikes me as more
of a king than a creator.

00:04:01.613 --> 00:04:03.531
And I'm not sure you can be both.

00:04:05.283 --> 00:04:06.993
As an experiment,

00:04:07.035 --> 00:04:11.456
I decided to remake the Book
of Genesis as a graphic novel.

00:04:13.374 --> 00:04:16.169
My version of God is not confident.

00:04:16.211 --> 00:04:19.339
And maybe not coincidentally,

00:04:19.339 --> 00:04:20.840
she's a woman.

00:04:23.176 --> 00:04:27.722
It surprised me how few changes
I needed to make to the original text,

00:04:27.722 --> 00:04:32.393
which is sparse and ancient
and lends itself well to interpretation.

00:04:33.228 --> 00:04:34.813
For example,

00:04:34.813 --> 00:04:38.149
the Bible opens in this
mysterious, moody way

00:04:38.191 --> 00:04:43.112
with God floating aimlessly on the face
of a dark, mysterious void.

00:04:46.366 --> 00:04:49.702
In my version, I have her
floating this way

00:04:49.744 --> 00:04:53.790
because she's feeling despondent
about her limitations as an artist.

00:04:54.624 --> 00:04:59.879
She's made this messy, wet, mixed up
dark first drafts of the world,

00:04:59.921 --> 00:05:02.298
and she just doesn't know
where to go from here.

00:05:06.553 --> 00:05:09.848
My version of God doesn't know
exactly what she's doing,

00:05:09.889 --> 00:05:14.269
but she draws a horizon line,
and things start to fall into place.

00:05:15.728 --> 00:05:19.232
She banishes Adam and Eve
from the Garden of Eden,

00:05:19.274 --> 00:05:21.734
not because they disobeyed her,

00:05:21.776 --> 00:05:25.280
but because, by eating the apple
and becoming wise,

00:05:25.321 --> 00:05:28.825
she feels they have outgrown
the world she created for them,

00:05:28.825 --> 00:05:30.743
and she needs to let them go.

00:05:32.912 --> 00:05:36.583
She scatters the builders
of the Tower of Babel,

00:05:36.624 --> 00:05:39.669
not because she's threatened
by their power,

00:05:39.669 --> 00:05:42.171
but because, like any introvert,

00:05:42.213 --> 00:05:43.798
she needs her privacy.

00:05:43.840 --> 00:05:46.718
(Laughter)

00:05:49.345 --> 00:05:52.473
When she destroys the world
in the story of Noah,

00:05:52.515 --> 00:05:56.561
it's not because she's
incensed with mankind,

00:05:56.561 --> 00:05:58.813
but because she's incensed with herself.

00:05:59.564 --> 00:06:02.400
She knows she could have done
a better job when she made us.

00:06:04.903 --> 00:06:07.614
My adaptation of the Book of Genesis

00:06:07.655 --> 00:06:11.534
is a creation story full
of false starts and absurdities.

00:06:13.119 --> 00:06:15.622
But it's a creation story all the same.

00:06:16.497 --> 00:06:19.417
One in which a self-conscious woman,

00:06:19.417 --> 00:06:22.712
even though she worries
and makes mistakes,

00:06:22.754 --> 00:06:26.382
is nonetheless a successful,
committed artist.

00:06:29.677 --> 00:06:31.137
When I finished my book,

00:06:31.137 --> 00:06:34.724
I did feel a new connection
to the God of the Torah

00:06:34.766 --> 00:06:37.560
and a new sense of belonging
to my religion.

00:06:39.062 --> 00:06:42.440
I also felt a new sense of belonging,

00:06:42.482 --> 00:06:43.608
period.

00:06:44.359 --> 00:06:48.571
It's lonely being someone
who has no idea how to act normal.

00:06:49.072 --> 00:06:52.033
But it's profoundly less lonely
being that person

00:06:52.033 --> 00:06:54.077
in a world created for her

00:06:54.077 --> 00:06:57.246
by an equally awkward, self-conscious God.

00:06:59.791 --> 00:07:00.959
These days,

00:07:01.000 --> 00:07:03.711
when I worry that I won't know
what to make for dinner,

00:07:03.711 --> 00:07:06.547
I remind myself that God
wouldn't know either.

00:07:07.006 --> 00:07:09.592
(Laughter)

00:07:11.135 --> 00:07:13.429
This picture is giving me anxiety.

00:07:13.471 --> 00:07:15.556
(Laughter)

00:07:15.598 --> 00:07:18.226
It's like, literally, I drew it from life.

00:07:19.435 --> 00:07:21.437
(Laughter)

00:07:23.481 --> 00:07:25.441
This gives me the confidence

00:07:25.441 --> 00:07:29.028
to embrace my cluelessness
and just wing it.

00:07:30.029 --> 00:07:31.614
Thank you, thank you so much.

00:07:31.656 --> 00:07:37.245
(Cheers and applause)

